NoteThe task: answer a population question from a national survey





The chapters in this part of the book walk through one complete analysis of complex survey data in R, from downloading a raw file to reporting a multivariable model:

importing a large survey file (this chapter);
data engineering: stacking survey files and joining a lookup table (Chapter 3);
cleaning and recoding with explicit checks (Chapter 4);
survey-weighted descriptive statistics (Chapter 5);
multiple imputation (MI) of missing data, with a discussion of survey-design assumptions (Chapter 6);
bivariate (crude) survey-weighted regression (Chapter 7);
multivariable (adjusted) survey-weighted regression, a sensitivity analysis, and reporting (Chapter 8).

The question. Is household income associated with reported diagnosed diabetes among adults in the participating BRFSS jurisdictions? Diabetes is common, costly, and unequally distributed. Income shapes access to healthy food, safe places to be active, health care, and the time and resources to manage a chronic condition. We estimate how the prevalence of diabetes differs across income groups, before and after accounting for age, sex, and race and ethnicity.
These chapters assume you have worked through Chapter 1, or are comfortable with dplyr verbs and joins, and that you know the basics of regression.

7.2 Fitting and pooling
Three short functions do the work for every model in this chapter and the next:

fit_each() takes each of the 5 completed datasets in turn (mice::complete(imputed, i)), adds the 0/1 diabetes outcome, turns the dataset into a survey design, and fits the model. It keeps only what pooling needs: the estimates, their covariance matrix, and the residual degrees of freedom. Survey designs and model objects can be large, so each is discarded after extracting these quantities rather than retained for all 5 datasets.
pool_fits() combines the 5 sets of estimates with Rubin’s rules, using mice::pool.table(), and exponentiates them into prevalence ratios.
format_pr() turns the pooled estimates into a readable table.



Show / hide code
# Fit one model per completed dataset; keep the estimates, their covariance matrix, and the
# residual degrees of freedom. Each design is built, used, and discarded in turn.
fit_each &lt;- function(imputed, formula) {
  purrr::map(seq_len(imputed$m), \(i) {
    design &lt;- mice::complete(imputed, i) |&gt;
      dplyr::mutate(diabetes_yes = as.integer(diabetes == "Yes")) |&gt;
      srvyr::as_survey_design(
        ids = psu_id,
        strata = strata,
        weights = weight,
        check_strata = FALSE
      )
    fit &lt;- survey::svyglm(
      formula,
      design = design,
      family = stats::quasipoisson()
    )
    list(
      coef = stats::coef(fit),
      vcov = stats::vcov(fit),
      df = stats::df.residual(fit)
    )
  })
}

pool_fits &lt;- function(fits) {
  estimates &lt;- purrr::map(fits, \(fit) {
    tibble::tibble(
      term = names(fit$coef),
      estimate = fit$coef,
      std.error = sqrt(diag(fit$vcov)),
      df.residual = fit$df
    )
  })
  mice::pool.table(purrr::list_rbind(estimates), exponentiate = TRUE)
}

format_pr &lt;- function(pooled) {
  pooled |&gt;
    tibble::as_tibble() |&gt;
    dplyr::filter(term != "(Intercept)") |&gt;
    dplyr::transmute(
      term,
      `PR (95% CI)` = sprintf(
        "%.2f (%.2f to %.2f)",
        estimate,
        conf.low,
        conf.high
      ),
      p = format.pval(p.value, digits = 2, eps = 0.001)
    )
}

8.1 Helpers
The crude prevalence ratios (PRs) in Chapter 7 mix the association of income with the associations of everything that travels with income. Age is the clearest example: adults 65 and older are less likely than middle-aged adults to have a high household income (many are retired) and far more likely to have diabetes. A multivariable model estimates the association of income with diabetes among adults of the same age group, sex, and race and ethnicity.
The three helper functions are the same as in Chapter 7.
