8.5 Reporting the results


Show / hide code
top_income &lt;- dplyr::filter(adjusted, term == "income$100,000 or more")
top_income_cc &lt;- dplyr::filter(
  complete_case_pr,
  term == "income$100,000 or more"
)


A results paragraph might read:

We analyzed 457,670 adults in the 2024 Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System, representing 263.8 million adults in the participating jurisdictions (49 states, DC, Guam, Puerto Rico, and the US Virgin Islands). The regression analyses accounted for survey weights, strata, and primary sampling units. Household income was missing for 19.1% of respondents, and missingness varied with age and general health. We multiply imputed missing values (5 imputations by chained equations with the mice package, version 3.19.0, predictive mean matching, with the survey weight as a predictor) and pooled estimates with Rubin’s rules. In a survey-weighted modified Poisson model adjusted for age group, sex, and race and ethnicity, adults with a household income of $100,000 or more had a prevalence of diabetes 0.47 times that of adults earning less than $25,000 (95% confidence interval [CI] 0.44 to 0.50; joint test of income p &lt;0.001). In a complete-case analysis the prevalence ratio was 0.47 (95% CI 0.44 to 0.50).

This is an illustrative analysis of cross-sectional, self-reported data. Adjustment does not make the association causal: illness can affect income, and unmeasured factors can affect both. The diabetes measure omits undiagnosed disease and pregnancy-only diabetes. The short imputation run and omitted geographic and stratum predictors need further evaluation before substantive reporting.

8.5.1 Reporting checklist
For any analysis of complex survey data, report:

the survey design (weights, strata, clusters) and that every estimate used it;
the unweighted sample size and the weighted population;
design-based tests (for example, Rao-Scott chi-square) and confidence intervals;
design effects for key estimates.

When the analysis uses multiple imputation, also report:

the amount of missing data for each variable and evidence about why it is missing;
the imputation method for each variable, the number of imputations, and the software and version;
the variables in the imputation model, including how the survey design entered it;
that estimates were pooled with Rubin’s rules;
complete-case results next to the imputed results.
